WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:09.568
When the Mouse heard Alice calling, he turned around and swam slowly toward her.

2
00:00:09.569 --> 00:00:13.890
With a trembling voice, he said, "Let's swim to shore.

3
00:00:13.891 --> 00:00:22.301
After I tell you my story, you'll understand why I hate cats and dogs."

4
00:00:22.302 --> 00:00:29.590
Alice was happy to get out of the salt water.

5
00:00:29.591 --> 00:00:33.073
The pool was getting crowded with birds.

6
00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:39.201
Alice led the way, and everyone swam to shore.

7
00:00:39.202 --> 00:00:43.422
It was an odd-looking group that gathered at the edge of the pool.

8
00:00:43.423 --> 00:00:49.998
Everyone, including Alice, was dripping wet, grumpy, and uncomfortable.

9
00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:53.782
The main question was how to get dry again.

10
00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:59.147
The creatures discussed this among themselves, and Alice soon joined in.

11
00:00:59.148 --> 00:01:03.510
She wasn't surprised anymore that she could talk to birds and animals.

12
00:01:03.511 --> 00:01:07.669
Or that they could talk to her.

13
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:13.993
In fact, she had a long argument with the Parrot.

14
00:01:13.994 --> 00:01:17.262
"We need towels," Alice finally said.

15
00:01:17.263 --> 00:01:19.252
"Lots and lots of towels.

16
00:01:19.253 --> 00:01:22.852
That's the fastest way to dry off."

17
00:01:22.853 --> 00:01:26.689
"You're completely wrong," the Parrot said.

18
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:31.073
"Birds never use towels."

19
00:01:31.074 --> 00:01:37.187
"Do you have a better idea?" Alice was getting tired of arguing about towels.

20
00:01:37.188 --> 00:01:43.489
"I'm older than you are, so I know more than you do," the Parrot replied.

21
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.395
"Really, Parrot? How old are you anyway?"

22
00:01:47.396 --> 00:01:54.491
But the Parrot refused to reveal her age, so that was the end of the argument.

23
00:01:54.492 --> 00:01:57.947
Fortunately the Mouse took charge of the group.

24
00:01:57.948 --> 00:02:03.637
"Sit down, everyone, and I'll tell you my story while you dry.

25
00:02:03.638 --> 00:02:08.458
My family has a long, sad history with cats and—"

26
00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:12.112
"Ugh," the Duck said with a shiver.

27
00:02:12.113 --> 00:02:14.790
"I beg your pardon," the Mouse said.

28
00:02:14.791 --> 00:02:16.820
"Did you say something?"

29
00:02:16.821 --> 00:02:21.119
But before the Duck could reply, the Dodo spoke up.

30
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:27.457
"I know how to get dry. We'll have a race!"

31
00:02:27.458 --> 00:02:32.000
Alice looked around the hall. "Where will we race?"

32
00:02:32.001 --> 00:02:36.426
The Dodo was already marking off a rough circle.

33
00:02:36.427 --> 00:02:45.585
"The exact shape of the course doesn't matter," he said when he finished.

34
00:02:45.586 --> 00:02:49.994
It was the strangest race that Alice had ever seen.

35
00:02:49.995 --> 00:02:56.003
There was no starting line because the Dodo let everyone start in different spots.

36
00:02:56.004 --> 00:03:04.358
And he didn't say, "On your mark, get set, go!"

37
00:03:04.359 --> 00:03:10.612
Instead the creatures started whenever they liked.

38
00:03:10.613 --> 00:03:19.610
When they'd been running for thirty minutes, the Dodo called out, "The race is over!"

39
00:03:19.611 --> 00:03:23.072
Everyone was now dry and happy.

40
00:03:23.073 --> 00:03:26.332
The birds gathered around the Dodo.

41
00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:30.237
"But who is the winner?" one asked.

42
00:03:30.238 --> 00:03:36.892
This was a puzzling question, which the Dodo thought about for a long time.

43
00:03:36.893 --> 00:03:47.277
At last he said, "Everybody has won, and all must have prizes!"

44
00:03:47.278 --> 00:03:53.585
"But who will give out the prizes?" a bird asked.

45
00:03:53.586 --> 00:03:59.184
"She will, of course!" The Dodo pointed to Alice.

46
00:03:59.185 --> 00:04:06.073
Everyone crowded around her, shouting, "Prizes! Prizes!"

47
00:04:06.074 --> 00:04:09.851
Alice had no idea what to do.

48
00:04:09.852 --> 00:04:15.747
But reaching into her pocket, she found a box of candies.

49
00:04:15.748 --> 00:04:22.384
Luckily, they'd stayed dry, and she had enough to give everyone one piece.

50
00:04:22.385 --> 00:04:26.367
However, there wasn't one left for herself.

51
00:04:26.368 --> 00:04:31.457
"But you must have a prize!" the Mouse insisted.

52
00:04:31.458 --> 00:04:34.620
"Of course," the Dodo replied.

53
00:04:34.621 --> 00:04:36.444
He turned to Alice.

54
00:04:36.445 --> 00:04:41.372
"What else do you have in your pocket?"

55
00:04:41.373 --> 00:04:44.458
"Only a thimble," Alice said.

56
00:04:44.459 --> 00:04:49.258
"Hand it over," the Dodo commanded her.

57
00:04:49.259 --> 00:04:56.104
They all crowded around Alice once again as the Dodo solemnly awarded her the thimble.

58
00:04:56.105 --> 00:05:03.250
After he had finished a short speech, everyone cheered.

59
00:05:03.251 --> 00:05:06.460
Alice thought the whole thing was silly.

60
00:05:06.461 --> 00:05:11.325
But the birds looked so serious that she didn't dare laugh.

61
00:05:11.326 --> 00:05:18.775
So she simply bowed, thinking about what strange creatures she'd met in the rabbit hole.

